# Copy to .env.local.
#
# There is no NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable in this app, deliberately. Everything here
# is a server secret, and a Firebase credential compiled into a browser bundle
# is not recoverable by rotating a key later. See src/lib/firestore.ts.

# --- Firestore ------------------------------------------------------------
# Set → the local emulator, no credentials needed. Unset → the real project,
# and then GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS is mandatory. Exactly the rule
# scripts/src/lib/firestore.ts and apps/console use, so the three cannot drift.
FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:8080
GCLOUD_PROJECT=kgc-conference-app-and-website

# Only when FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST is unset.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/serviceAccount.json

# --- Order confirmation links --------------------------------------------
# Signs the capability token in /order/{token}. Required: the confirmation page
# shows a claim code, and the registration id it would otherwise be keyed by is
# derived from the email address and therefore guessable. Any random string of
# 16+ characters; rotating it invalidates outstanding confirmation links.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
WEB_ORDER_SECRET=change-me-openssl-rand-base64-32

# --- Public unsubscribe links ---------------------------------------------
# Signs the capability token in /u/{token}, the public unsubscribe link that
# every bulk campaign is legally required to carry. Optional: when unset it
# falls back to WEB_ORDER_SECRET, so a deployment that has not set it still
# sends working unsubscribe links rather than none.
#
# Set it anyway, and then never rotate it. Rotating WEB_ORDER_SECRET is a
# supported operation — it invalidates outstanding confirmation links, which is
# an acceptable cost. Rotating this one breaks the unsubscribe link in every
# email already sitting in somebody's inbox, and an unsubscribe link that
# 404s is what a spam complaint looks like just before it happens.
#
# Must be the same value here and on the organizer dashboard: the dashboard
# mints these links and this site verifies them.
# WEB_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=change-me-openssl-rand-base64-32

# --- Consent and release signing links -------------------------------------
# Signs the capability token in /consent/{token}, the link a speaker or attendee
# follows to sign a release without holding an account. Optional: when unset it
# falls back to WEB_ORDER_SECRET, and the dashboard's consent register says so
# on screen rather than minting links that silently fail to verify.
#
# The rotation cost sits between the other two. An outstanding signing link that
# stops verifying is recoverable — the organizer re-sends it — but a signature
# already stored is not re-signed, so rotating mid-collection splits the roster
# into people who signed under the old secret and people who cannot.
#
# Must be the same value here and on the organizer dashboard: the dashboard
# mints these links and this site verifies them.
# WEB_CONSENT_SECRET=change-me-openssl-rand-base64-32

# --- The temporary password handed out on purchase -------------------------
# Every account created by a ticket purchase is given SIX RANDOM DIGITS of its
# own. The value is shown on the /order/{token} confirmation page, mailed in the
# receipt, and stored on registrations/{rid}.tempPassword so the page can read
# it back — it is random, so it cannot be recomputed.
#
# It is not meant to survive. mustChangePassword is stamped on the profile, the
# app refuses every route until the attendee replaces it, and the stored copy is
# swept the next time the confirmation page is opened after that.
#
# Set to 0 (or false) to switch the whole thing off: no password is set, nothing
# is stored, nothing is printed, and the six-digit OTP code is the only way in.
# WARNING: those callables are NOT deployed, so switching this off currently
# means buyers cannot sign in at all. Anything other than 0/false leaves it on.
# ISSUE_TEMPORARY_PASSWORDS=1

# --- Stripe (REQUIRED to sell anything) -----------------------------------
# There is no fallback. With STRIPE_SECRET_KEY unset, /tickets says so on the
# page and startCheckout refuses before it reads a tier — an unconfigured site
# declines to sell rather than handing out free tickets. The card boxes on the
# checkout form stay visible and are disabled.
#
# Use a test key (sk_test_…) for anything that is not the real conference.
# Test mode moves no money and charges no fees.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
#
# From `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3200/api/stripe/webhook`, or from
# the endpoint's page in the Stripe dashboard.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# --- Email (Resend) -------------------------------------------------------
# Leave RESEND_API_KEY unset and no transactional email is sent. That is a
# supported state, not a broken one: every attempt is still written to the
# `emailLog` collection with status `skipped`, so the dashboard shows plainly
# that nothing went out rather than implying it did.
#
# Get the key at https://resend.com/api-keys (starts `re_`).
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
#
# MUST be a domain verified at https://resend.com/domains, or every send is
# rejected with 403. A friendly name matters more than it looks — "KGC 2027" is
# recognised in an inbox list and a bare address is not.
# EMAIL_FROM=KGC 2027 <tickets@knowledgegraph.tech>
#
# Where "just reply to this email" lands. Defaults to hello@knowledgegraph.tech.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@knowledgegraph.tech

# --- Deployment -----------------------------------------------------------
# This site's own origin. Two things read it and they behave differently, so
# both are worth knowing:
#
#   - The canonical URLs — `metadataBase` for Open Graph and `canonicalOrigin()`
#     for the schema.org/Event block — resolve it through `publicSiteOrigin()`
#     in @kgc/shared, which derives NOTHING from the request. A cached page
#     behind a proxy must not mint a canonical URL from a Host header somebody
#     else chose, so unset means the production default,
#     https://www.knowledgegraph.tech, never localhost. It defaulted to
#     localhost until 2026-09-01, which put `http://localhost:3200` into the one
#     format crawlers are meant to believe.
#   - Stripe's return URL (`siteOrigin()` in lib/stripe.ts) prefers this and
#     falls back to the request's Host, so checkout returns to whatever host the
#     buyer actually used. That is the one place a request-derived origin is
#     right, because it is a redirect back to a live session.
#
# So: set it in every deployed environment, and set it locally to
# http://localhost:3200 if you want canonical URLs that resolve on your machine.
# WEB_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://www.knowledgegraph.tech
